<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xin dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/9">9</a>–<a href="/years/23">23</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Xin dynasty was the short-lived regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>, who usurped the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in 9 CE and proclaimed a &quot;new&quot; (xin) dynasty. His reforms, including the nationalisation of land, a ban on the sale of slaves and repeated changes of currency, caused disorder, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> floods and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a> rebellion spread revolt. Wang Mang was killed when rebels took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 23, and the Han was restored under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu</a> in 25.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xin-dynasty.jpg" alt="Xin dynasty" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xin_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> the ruling Liu family was beset by factional struggles, and the Wang family grew powerful under Emperor Cheng of Han. Its leading member, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>, acted as regent for several young puppet emperors and presented himself as a champion of Confucian virtues. After the death of Emperor Ping of Han in 6 CE he secured control of the empire and crushed rebellions in 6 and 7 CE. Two years later he usurped the throne and proclaimed the Xin dynasty, named for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of which he had been Marquess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> remade the Han government on his reading of the Confucian classics and of the ancient Western Zhou system. To weaken the nobles and help the peasants he redistributed land from the rich to those who owned none, limited the farmland a family could hold, taxed slave owning and prohibited the sale of slaves. He nationalised farmland, restored the state monopolies on salt and iron and replaced the gold currency with bronze. A 10% tax on the net earnings of gathering, fishing, herding and trade was abolished in 22 CE after it caused discontent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> deteriorated, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> kept 300,000 soldiers on the northern border, which drained the empire's resources. A change in the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> caused massive floods, and locust plagues and famines followed. Peasants in the east turned to banditry, and the strongest bands became the rebel armies known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a>. They allied with discontented nobles and descendants of the Liu family, and by 19 CE there was a large-scale civil war.</p><p>Han restorationist armies completely defeated the Xin armies at the Battle of Kunyang in June or July 23 CE, and in October militias under Zhuang Ben and Zhuang Chun captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>. In 25 CE Liu Xiu was crowned Emperor Guangwu of Han at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows">Red Eyebrows</a> in 27 CE, and by 37 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> was fully restored.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xin dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tributary relations lapsed under Huduershi (18–48 CE), who took advantage of the upheavals of the <b>Xin dynasty</b> to regain the Western Regions and at the height of his power compared himself to Modu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Guangwu of Han, who had risen against Wang Mang's <b>Xin dynasty</b> and restored the Han, declared Luoyang the capital of the Eastern Han dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Lülin peasant rebellion, rebels captured and sacked Chang'an, capital of Wang Mang's short-lived <b>Xin dynasty</b>; Wang Mang was killed two days later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebel forces broke into Chang'an and killed Wang Mang, the usurper who had founded the <b>Xin dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As the <b>Xin</b> collapsed he seized the region, took the title King of Shu and in 25 proclaimed himself emperor of Chengjia, with his capital at Chengdu, the same year Liu Xiu restored the Han.</blockquote><a href="/state/chengjia">Chengjia</a></li></ul></section>
